text:
@vinaysurendran3338 In the early years (5 years ago) I mostly have made my money making high-quality websites in all kinds of fields. Lawyers, automotive repairs and parts, septics and home improvement and construction, grave stones, hotels, real estate, etc. etc. Then I got recommended, was found on ads by 3 companies - a logistics company, a home blueprint and construction (architecture), and an interesting company that rents spaces (halls for yoga lesson, dancing, and for psychology sessions, etc.) per hour. I've built and maintain their CRMs - customer management, payments, scheduling. For the logistics company I've built their graphic and their pricelist. I pretty much helped build these companies. Now I maintain them, I get assignments from them but not as much. My tech role for all of these is pretty much the go-to coder guy. I get the *I want/need such-and-such* from the client, I interpret it into an actual task, do it and send the bill for my work (I charge per hour of my time).

My earnings went down because I have not got a SINGLE new customer for a side gig or a new major project this whole entire year. I used to get a lot of side simple and fast stuff. One time I was paid for being a consultant for this online sports betting company - I helped them set-up Docker. Simply got paid for the expertise...well...no more. I just don't get paid for stuff that I would excel at or stuff that only I and a handful of people in the region knew how to do, because AI simply made human intelligence and expertise near obsolete. There are people out there with Computer Science degrees that can't find job. I'm just a self-taught guy.

So yeah, I can't really tell you what's in demand now. I haven't got any new work since like March 2026. I'm just working with the 3 companies that I've worked with for years now and desperately working on my own project while I still can afford to and before these companies just run out of assignments to give me.

@karo2090 You are dead wrong.  Are you seriously claiming there was more creativity spinning thread and weaving cloth and sewing grey clothes than there was in setting up and running automated looms?  Do you really think that washing clothes by hand was more creative and less boring than being a telephone operator or typist?

When spinning, weaving, and sewing were automated, that freed up 90% of women's time.  When washing machines and vacuums came along, they freed up even more time.  Women got out of the home and earned money as telephone operators and typists -- do you seriously believe those jobs were less creative and boring than spinning, weaving, sewing, and washing clothes?

You don't know what you are talking about.  If you think of typing and switchboard operator at all, you're thinking of them from a 2026 perspective, not the perspective of women who no longer spent their waking hours spinning, weaving , sewing, and washing.

@flo.motion Thanks for your reply!

Yes, FBX files created in Mixamo can be set to loop.
However, with a walking animation, the motion loops but the character doesn't continue moving forward—it just returns to its original position. Is there a good way to handle this?

@arm-k7d

Autoregression is an architectural choice, not a fundamental limitation of intelligence. AI can already process multimodal information and perform parallel computations. And claiming AI can never achieve consciousness or human-level reasoning assumes scientific conclusions we haven’t established, that’s philosophy at this point and is irrelevant for what AI could functionally do in the near-future. 

There are also diffusion models that can work on many parts of a generation at once without doing them sequentially.
